WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.246
File type validation should use multiple signals.

2
00:00:03.246 --> 00:00:11.726
File extension, MIME type, content type header, file signature, metadata, and parser results each provide different information.

3
00:00:11.726 --> 00:00:17.358
None of them alone proves that a file is safe or appropriate for a business workflow.

4
00:00:17.358 --> 00:00:21.333
Allow-list permitted file categories based on business need.

5
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:27.030
If a support case only needs document attachments, do not accept unrelated categories.

6
00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:31.999
If a profile feature only needs images, keep the allowed categories narrow.

7
00:00:31.999 --> 00:00:38.823
Business purpose should drive what the endpoint accepts, not what the platform can technically receive.

8
00:00:38.823 --> 00:00:43.924
Browser-provided labels and client metadata are useful hints, not guarantees.

9
00:00:43.924 --> 00:00:54.193
A content type header, display name, or extension can help route a validation decision, but the application should not treat those labels as authoritative.

10
00:00:54.193 --> 00:01:00.023
Server-side validation should decide whether the file matches what the workflow expects.

11
00:01:00.023 --> 00:01:01.878
Validate more than category.

12
00:01:01.878 --> 00:01:12.015
Size, structure, expected format, required fields, image dimensions, document properties, processing requirements, and import constraints may all matter.

13
00:01:12.015 --> 00:01:19.700
A file can be the right general type and still be too large, malformed, incomplete, or unsuitable for the operation.

14
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:25.663
Validation outcomes should be consistent across upload, preview, processing, and download.

15
00:01:25.663 --> 00:01:32.685
A file that is rejected for normal processing should not later become previewable through a separate path.

16
00:01:32.685 --> 00:01:38.515
A file allowed for private storage should not become public through a different handler.

17
00:01:38.515 --> 00:01:40.967
Consistency prevents state confusion.

18
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:42.689
Handle metadata carefully.

19
00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:51.302
Metadata can contain names, locations, titles, device information, comments, or other details that may be sensitive or misleading.

20
00:01:51.302 --> 00:02:00.113
Decide what metadata to retain, strip, display, index, or log based on the file category, user expectation, and privacy requirements.
